---
import ServiceLayout from '../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../components/ComparisonTable.astro';
import { faqs } from '../config/faqs';

const description =
	'Business websites on GitHub and Cloudflare with source-code ownership, fast delivery, and no traditional monthly hosting fee for qualifying static sites.';
---

<ServiceLayout
	title="Website Design & Ownership"
	description={description}
	eyebrow="Websites & digital marketing"
	heading="Own your website. Not just your login."
	lede="A business site should bring in work, load quickly, and remain yours. The architecture is GitHub and Cloudflare Pages — source code you can hold, delivered from a global network."
	crumb="Website Solutions"
	serviceName="Website design and web infrastructure"
	serviceType="Website design"
	faqs={faqs.website}
	ctaLabel="Build a Better Website"
	ctaTitle="Build a better website."
	ctaText="Tell us what the business needs the site to do. The stack follows from that, not from a template catalog."
	related={[
		{
			href: '/unifi-networking/',
			label: 'UniFi networking',
			text: 'The same preference for systems you control, applied to the building.',
		},
		{
			href: '/contact/',
			label: 'Discuss a site',
			text: 'Share the business, the current site, and what should change.',
		},
		{
			href: '/about/',
			label: 'How projects run',
			text: 'Consultation, design, deployment, verification, and support.',
		},
	]}
>
	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="problem-heading">Most small-business sites are rented.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					A proprietary page builder, a hosting subscription, a plugin pile, and a database you are not equipped to
					maintain. The login works until the platform changes its prices, its editor, or its mind. Moving the site
					later becomes a reconstruction project.
				</p>
				<p>
					That arrangement is convenient to sell. It is a poor way to own the one piece of digital property a
					business depends on.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream" aria-labelledby="outcome-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="outcome-heading">What you leave with.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					A fast site, structured so people and search engines can tell what the business does. Lead forms that post
					to a secure endpoint, not to a secret buried in the page. Pages that read clearly on a phone. And the
					source code, in a repository you can access, with a history of every change.
				</p>
				<p>
					If a release misbehaves, it can be rolled back. If you later want another developer, or another host, the
					code is portable. The site is not a tenant of the tool that built it.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="how-heading">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="split">
				<div>
					<h2 id="how-heading">GitHub and Cloudflare, in ordinary language.</h2>
				</div>
				<p class="lede">Two tools. One publishes the work. The other delivers it.</p>
			</div>
			<ul class="index-list">
				<li>
					<strong>GitHub</strong>
					<p>
						The workshop. Source files live in a repository with version history, so changes are reviewed and earlier
						versions can be restored. Visitors never need to know it is there.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Cloudflare Pages</strong>
					<p>
						The delivery. The built site is served from Cloudflare’s network, close to the person opening it, with
						HTTPS handled for the site. There is no traditional hosting account and no database running the pages.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Your domain</strong>
					<p>
						People still type your address. The domain is registered wherever you choose to register it. Registration
						is a separate cost. It is not included in the claim that hosting fees go away.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream" aria-labelledby="included-heading">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2 id="included-heading">What the architecture is built to do.</h2>
			<ul class="index-list">
				<li>
					<strong>Ownership</strong>
					<p>Full source-code ownership and repository access, with Git history and a straightforward rollback.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Speed</strong>
					<p>Static pages delivered from Cloudflare’s edge. Performance is a property of the build, not a plugin.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Security</strong>
					<p>
						No database and no plugin runtime on the public site, which removes entire classes of common compromises.
						That is a smaller attack surface. It is not a promise that any website is beyond attack.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>HTTPS</strong>
					<p>Certificates for the site are issued and renewed through Cloudflare. Visitors get a secure connection by default.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Search</strong>
					<p>
						Clear structure, unique titles, metadata, and room for local relevance. Analytics can be added when you
						want measurement. Rankings are not guaranteed.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Leads</strong>
					<p>
						Forms for consultation and inquiry, connected through a secure endpoint. Google Business Profile alignment
						and local pages are available when there is something real to say.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Craft</strong>
					<p>Mobile-first layout, a professional workflow, and a codebase another competent developer can read.</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
			<aside class="note">
				<p>
					This architecture eliminates the need for a traditional monthly web-hosting package on qualifying static
					sites. Domains, email, optional analytics, third-party APIs, and other external services are not implied to
					be free.
				</p>
			</aside>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="compare-heading">
		<div class="wrap stack">
			<div class="split">
				<div>
					<h2 id="compare-heading">A quieter stack.</h2>
				</div>
				<p class="lede">
					The comparison is about dependency and ownership. It is not a claim that every older site is unsafe, or
					that a static site needs no care.
				</p>
			</div>
			<ComparisonTable />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream" aria-labelledby="move-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="move-heading">Freedom to leave is part of the design.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					Portable source, minimal dependencies, and a static build mean the site can be hosted somewhere else.
					Cloudflare is an excellent default. It is not a lock. The repository is the asset. The host is a choice.
				</p>
				<p>
					Marketing support — search, writing, and the unglamorous work of keeping a business visible — can sit
					alongside the build when it earns its place. It is not bolted on as a mandatory retainer.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</ServiceLayout>
